  "query_preview": "..Today...Limited moisture across much of northern \nArizona will keep the threat for storms today fairly low. Much \nlike yesterday, coverage looks to remain fairly isolated, and \ngenerally focused over the higher terrain. A weak wave does look \nto move up the Colorado River Valley today, which may help to \npromote a little more coverage across western Arizona given the \nadded upward motion. Some brief heavy downpours and Gusty outflow \nwinds will be the main threats from any stronger storm that \ndevelops today.\n \nMonday and Tuesday...High pressure slowly begins to retrograde \nand strengthen over the southwest through the coming week. Warming\ntemperatures ensue across northern Arizona as a result, with \nafternoon highs returning to near-normal to slightly (~5F) above \nnormal.\n\nWith monsoonal moisture still in place, precipitation chances look \nto still continue into the the start of the week. However, as the\nridge strengthens, afternoon convection will be suppressed. \nTherefore, much of the activity looks to remain isolated in nature\nand mainly over the higher terrain. The best chances each day \nwill mainly be from the eastern Mogollon Rim into the White \nMountains.\n\nWednesday through Saturday...By the later half of the week, the \nhigh begins to move into a more favorable position as it wobbles \naround the southwest. Guidance is still a little shaky on the \nexact positioning and strength of the high, so some uncertain in \nexact coverage and locations remains. Nevertheless, coverage does \nlook to tick back upwards by the end of the week.",
